cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this artifact, please cite both the paper and the archived artifact."
title: "When a Judgment Layer's Self-Reported Fields Lie"
abstract: >-
  A measurement and characterisation study of three judgment layers -- a local
  non-autoregressive typed-decision model (Laya), a remote typed-decision service
  (TypeSafe Jev), and a frontier autoregressive model (DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash) -- on a
  common item set. Four claims are tested; three hold independently (cost is not the
  binding constraint; access-layer self-reported fields are not trustworthy; capability
  collapses on tasks requiring the detection of an absence) and the fourth -- that a
  heterogeneous judge provides incremental coverage over the generator -- is NOT
  supported in any of three task regimes.
type: software
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/PerryLink/llm-jev-laya-bench"
# The author publishes under a pen name rather than a legal name. This block, the byline
# printed in both PDFs, and the Zenodo deposit record must all give the SAME string, or a
# reader who cites the paper and a reader who cites the record are citing two different
# names.
#
# History: this block said `name: "PerryLink"` while the PDFs said "Perry Link". The
# mismatch was found while filling in the Zenodo form. `name:` was chosen originally to
# avoid Zenodo rendering the handle twice as "PerryLink, PerryLink", which is a real
# problem -- but the fix for it is given/family names, not a divergent spelling.
# The handle is still recorded as an alias, so GitHub's own citation widget, which derives
# from this file, keeps the same string.
authors:
  - family-names: "Link"
    given-names: "Perry"
    alias: "PerryLink"
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
keywords:
  - LLM evaluation
  - judgment layer
  - calibration
  - negative results
  - reproducibility
  - typed decisions
  - measurement
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22901248"
version: "v1.0.2"
date-released: "2026-09-22"

# The artifact's CONCEPT DOI, which always resolves to the latest release. The version DOI
# for v1.0.2 is 10.5281/zenodo.22901355 and its GitHub release tag is v1.0.2.
#
# THE PAPERS ARE NOT THIS RECORD. Their DOIs are listed under `identifiers` below rather
# than in `doi`, because `doi` means "the DOI of THIS software or dataset" and putting a
# paper DOI there would make the artifact claim to be the paper. That is the same
# wrong-object attribution this repository keeps finding -- a correct identifier attached
# to the wrong thing.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22901853
    description: >-
      The paper about this artifact, English original. Cite this for the measurements;
      cite this record for the code and the results\ artifacts.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22902025
    description: >-
      Chinese translation of the paper. The English text and the translation are language
      versions of ONE piece of work, not two papers -- cite one, not both. Where they
      differ, the English text governs.
  - type: url
    value: "https://github.com/PerryLink/llm-jev-laya-bench"
    description: "Source code repository (the artifact's development home)."
